Development Notes for my hockey-all repository

	This is a repository I am modifying, mostly with the my-mods branch.


Function and variable renames
	pbp_expand()
		could rename it to pbp_enhance() as expand is usually a data operation to convert a df to all the possible combination of the specified variables.
		also do we need expand and index to be seperate functions? Could the index blocks be move to be in expand/enhance?


Blocked shots
	Block shots are interestingly recorded
		- the event_team is the team that blocked the shot and the initial details.zoneCode is always 'D' but we change it in expand().
		- thhe coords_* are from the spot the shot was blocked, not where the shot was made -- we don't know where the shot originated from.

	For fenwick events, this means that we need to change the event_zone so that it aligns with the event_team. Otherwise, doesn't make much sense to have an event for a team that makes the shot happen in the Defensive zone.

	In a way, a blocked shot is 2 events in one: a shot attempt by the offensive team (in the offensive zone -- not really a blocked shot if it came from outside the zone), and a block shot by the defensive team.

	But we mostly want to treat it as a shot attempt -- which does not preclude us from the also counting blocked shots from the perspective of the defensive team. We could also split the event into 2 events --  one for the shot and one for the block -- if we wanted to. The Python data also provide info on who made the shot and who blocked it.

	The only info we don't have are the coordinates from where the shot was made; the coords are those of where the shot was blocked.


homeTeamDefendingSide
	Describe it, see event_distance calculation description and move relevant comments here and refer back to this section, in the event_distance calculation description.


priority and sortOrder
	The priority col:
		. is added in devpynhl (nhl_fetch.py) to sort the df after the play-by-play (events data) is merged with the change events. The sortOrder is provided by the json data.
	sortOrder col:
		. provided With the python / json data so we can/should also use it to sort/index the data -- but is not defined for our chnage events.
	event_index col:
		. in fun.pbp_expand() we create the event_index col to have an index of all events per game_id but when looking at the order based on the priority col, I noticed a few oddities.
		. I also retain the original order, not sure that it matters -- would only affect my change events I think

	If we need to continue to modify the priority column, it would be in pbp_expand() -- and eventually in nhl_fetch once we are confident that we have an order that works.


	In nhl_fetch.py, we create it as:

    df_enhanced_events[ 'priority' ] = (
        1  * (ser_event.isin( [ 'takeaway', 'giveaway', 'missed-shot', 'hit', "shot-on-goal", 'blocked-shot']) & ~( (ser_period == 5) & (session == "R" ))) +
        2  * ((ser_event == "goal")             & ~( (ser_period == 5) & (session == "R"))) +
        3  * ((ser_event == "stoppage")         & ~( (ser_period == 5) & (session == "R"))) +
        4  * ((ser_event == "challenge")        & ~( (ser_period == 5) & (session == "R"))) +
        5  * ((ser_event == "delayed-penalty")  & ~( (ser_period == 5) & (session == "R"))) +
        6  * ((ser_event == "penalty")          & ~( (ser_period == 5) & (session == "R"))) +
        7  * ((ser_event == "period-end")       & ~( (ser_period == 5) & (session == "R"))) +
        8  * ((ser_event == "game-end")         & ~( (ser_period == 5) & (session == "R"))) +
        9  * ((ser_event == "change-off")       & ~( (ser_period == 5) & (session == "R"))) +
        10 * ((ser_event == "change-on")        & ~( (ser_period == 5) & (session == "R"))) +
        11 * ((ser_event == "period-start")     & ~( (ser_period == 5) & (session == "R"))) +
        12 * ((ser_event == "faceoff")          & ~( (ser_period == 5) & (session == "R")))
        )

	The hit (priority 1) and shot-on-goal (1), hit (1) and takeaway (1) are consistent with the values of sortOrder in a few spots. Also stoppage (priority 3) and penalty (6) sometimes -- also, I think that penalty equates to a stoppage but cannot be certain TODO check how frequent they happen individually and together.

	I find it odd that delayed-penalty was after stoppage: in the same game_second, wouldn't it impossible to have a delayed-penalty after a stoppage? and if they occur at the same time, would still make sense to treat the delayed penalty as having occurred prior to the stoppage.

	For a moment I thougth that changing the priority for stoppage to 7 could solve it but it doesn't, as sometimes there is penalty then stoppage, sometimes vice-versa (according to sortOrder). So then I thougth that perhaps penalties and stoppage should have the same priority (i.e. 6) and we would let sortOrder determine the proper order. We would not need to change any priority for events that have the same priority (sortOrder) would take care of it.

	See in fun.pbp_expand() how I am currently ordering the priority.

