		General comment about fun.pbp_expand_Rscraped_data()
			I should take fun.pbp_expand_Rscraped_data() with a grain of salt i.e. it is far from perfect. In replicating it as fun.pbp_expand() for the Python data, I used it to create the cols required to reproduce the XG model but changed many of them (e.g. event_distance) -- it is pretty badly and sadly coded.

		coords_x, coords_y
			These cols already exist in the data -- recall that coords_x is the length of the ice, coords_y is the width. The middle of either is 0.

			coords_x -- whether it represents a teams Off or Def zone changes each period
				2025 09 13 update: TODO: reconsider/rewrite the description of what is below -- it is a bit old and not exactly accurate i.e. positive values of coords_x do not always denote the Defensive zone of the home team, it can change from one game to another. The Python data has a column named homeTeamDefendingSide containing the values of either lef/right (from the perspective of the scorekeepers' box I think) that changes across periods. If we didn't have it we could construct it based on average values of the coords_x in the 1st and 3rd period for the home team -- positive would mean 'right' and negative would mean 'left'.

				At the beginning of the game, positive x <=> home team's Defensive zone.

				If we do
					table( pbp_part$coords_x, pbp_part$event_zone )
				we see that -24 to 24 correspond to the Neu zone. And absolute values 25 to 99 correspond to the Def or Off zones.

				Taking a game as an example, i.e. 2021030111 WSH at FLA, the first shot attempt that is not in the neutral zone is from Verhaeghe (a MISS that goes wide). The event_team is FLA so the event_zone is offensive (as we would expect) and the x coordinate is negative (-49). The next shot if from WAS, event_zone is again Off (as we would expect for a shot) and coords_x is now positive. All of the WSH shots in the 1st period have coords_x > 0. All of the FLA shots in the 1st period have a coords < 0.

				So in the first period, positive x coordinates denote FLA's Def zone <=> WAS's Off zone. Since FLA is the home team, the game would seem to start with positive coords x being the Defensive zone of the home team. If we move the 2nd period, the first shot if from FLA (Montour) and the x coord is now positive.  This makes sense, teams switch side after the first period.

				So every odd (even) numbered period, postive  x => Def of the home (away) team.
				So every odd (even) numbered period, negative x => Off of the home (away) team.

				Think hard about how to summarize this and ensure it's correct: so whether the event_zone is Off or Def based on the x coords depends on the period and which team's perspective the event is defined as (a FAC won, a HIT, a MISS, a BLOCK).

				I eyeballed the above above with:
					my_cols = c( 'game_id', 'home_team', 'game_period', 'event_description', 'event_team', 'event_zone', 'event_type', 'coords_x', 'coords_y', 'event_distance', 'pbp_distance', 'event_circle', 'event_angle' )
					mask = pbp_part$event_type != 'CHANGE'
					tmp <- pbp_part[ mask, my_cols]
					mask2 = tmp$game_id == 2021030111
					View( tmp[ mask2, c( 'game_period', 'home_team', 'event_description', 'event_team', 'event_type', 'event_zone', 'coords_x') ] )

