			TODO: will have to check if the values are consistent with the R data version e.g. for blocked shots.

		Coordinates and distance, angle calculations
			The way I think about it, for the purpose of calculating shot distance and angle, the rink is a rectangle that 89 x 2 feet long (e.g. 188 feet) and 85 feet wide.
				https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Hockey_League_rules

			Then, we can imagine and area that get extend beyond those to be 11 feet in the negative, which would be beyond the goal line (i.e then end of that rectangle). For distance calculation, we would take the absolute value for these shots and the same y coordinate would apply.

			For long shots, the approach that the Evolving dudes have taken to modify the distance and angle can be simplified.

			The issue is simple: since the x coordinate is 0 at center ice we need to adjust that coordinate when shots are made from beyond the centre red line.

			So before we even compute the distance and angle, I considered 2 approaches:

			i) one that creates and adjusted coords_x column (adjusted for long shots)
			ii) create a col representing feet from the goal line instead of a coordinate, we would just then use feet in the distance and angle calculation
			- for coords_y, it is already in feet in relation to center of the ice, which also corresponds to feet from the middle of the net on the goal line -- so we already have a perfectly usable feet column for y

			Feet from the goal line would be 89 - abs( coords_x) except that we need to augment it for long shots.

			How do we identify which rows need to augmented?  I initially thought if could be as follows
				df$is_long_shot = df$event_type %in% st.fenwick_events & df$event_zone == 'D'
			but this misses long shots from the defensive side of the neutral zone. We would also need to make that boolean be TRUE for fenwick event from the defensive side of neutral zone.

			It is when the sign of the x is not what we would expect depending on which net a team shots on in a given period.

			I had initially coded this as how it appears below but the final version that works in the code is much simpler. See the code xG_preparation.py
				df$fenwick_x_sign_home = ifelse( df$homeTeamDefendingSide == 'right', -1, 1 )
				df$fenwick_x_sign_away = -1 * df$fenwick_x_sign_home
				df$fenwick_x_sign_home = ifelse( df$homeTeamDefendingSide == 'right', 1 - (2 * (df$period %% 2)), -1 + (2 * (df$period %% 2)) )
				# ... in the event that we no longer have homeTeamDefendingSide, we could take the average coords_x from the 1st and 3rd period to get the expected sign starting from period 1 and then apply a modulus function base on period (teams change sides each period including in regular season overtime)

				df$is_long_shot_alt = ifelse( df$event_type %in% st.fenwick_events,
											  ifelse ( df$event_team==df$home_team,
													   df$coords_x * df$fenwick_x_sign_home < 0,
													   df$coords_x * df$fenwick_x_sign_away < 0 ),
											  FALSE )

			I am keeping the use of the modulus function as in the code above just in case -- i.e. in case we no longer have the homeTeamDefendingSide column, we would the compute the average of the home team's coord_x in the first and period; the sign of that average would be the expectes sign for the home team in the first period. And sign teams switch side after each period, including the regular season overtime, we would use the modulus function applied to the period to create the fenwick_x_sign column.

			Shots behind the net:
				The distance is fine for events from behind the goal line as the distance turns negative beyond the goal line up to the board and we take the absolute value for the distance calculation.

				I wonder if the angle makes sense though? The angle just < 90 immediately after the gl and gets smaller as abs( coords_x ) increases or abs( coords_y ) decreases. Is that what we want? i.e. would it behave in the same direction in terms of effect on an expected goal (intuitively speaking), i.e. a smaller angle should make for a better shot right? It's also possible that the angle behind the net would not matter much -- the model should probably just know that a given shot if from behind the net.

				See .Rhistory for # 2025 09 12 -- looking into event_angle for shots behind the net: I inspected the data and we get identical distance and angle for those shots compared with the R-scraped 20212022 playoff data.

		home_zonestart
			I had initially "translated" to data.table syntax the tidyverse block that creates the home_zonestart in a subset df and then joins it, but realized... why create a subset data set and join? Perhaps that's the tidyverse way but all we are doing is create a new column here, albeit by groups but that's easy to do with data.table's. I just want to keep the code for how I initially translated it. I had:

			df_fo = as.data.table( df )
			df_fo$mask = df_fo$event_type %in% c(st.corsi_events, 'faceoff') & ! df_fo$is_shoot_out
			df_fo[ mask==TRUE, face_index := cumsum(event_type == 'faceoff') ]
			df_fo[ mask==TRUE,
			`:=`(
			  test = first(home_zone),
			  home_zonestart = ifelse(first(home_zone) == 'D', 1,
									   ifelse(first(home_zone) == 'N', 2,
											  ifelse(first(home_zone) == 'O', 3, NA)))
			  ),
			by = .(game_id, face_index) ]
			df_fo <- df_fo[ mask==TRUE, .(game_id, event_index, home_zonestart) ]
			# identical( df_fo$home_zonestart, face_index$home_zonestart )
			# . we get identical cols if we do period < 5 below but the above is actually correct: in playoffs, we want all overtime periods, even the 5th and beyond obviously
			# df_fo$mask = df_fo$event_type %in% c(st.corsi_events, 'faceoff') & df_fo$period < 5

			df <- left_join(df, df_fo, by = c('game_id', 'event_index'))

			See how I currently have it in the function. I just create the new col and there is no need for a subsequent join.

